1
00:00:00,665 --> 00:00:03,805
Today, let's read a line of early Swift.

2
00:00:04,425 --> 00:00:05,595
We start at four.

3
00:00:06,215 --> 00:00:11,285
With the plus signs at the end, which number gets saved?

4
00:00:12,625 --> 00:00:17,215
It saves four first, then raises the original variable to five.

5
00:00:17,805 --> 00:00:22,325
With the signs in front, it would increment first and save five.

6
00:00:23,675 --> 00:00:27,895
Chris Lattner’s earliest surviving proposal record dates to November 2015.

7
00:00:28,515 --> 00:00:31,415
It already says “Accepted.”

8
00:00:32,035 --> 00:00:36,175
The proposal also considered removing just the return value.

9
00:00:36,795 --> 00:00:40,635
Then putting the signs before or after the variable would do the same thing.

10
00:00:41,255 --> 00:00:47,755
With a one-line increment already available, two more spellings add little.

11
00:00:49,105 --> 00:00:52,925
That December, David Zarzycki took the idea further.

12
00:00:53,545 --> 00:00:59,255
Could user-defined postfix operators go too?

13
00:00:59,875 --> 00:01:02,755
Jonathan Hull had working code.

14
00:01:03,375 --> 00:01:09,035
In his grammar, a trailing star meant a rule could repeat zero or more times.

15
00:01:09,655 --> 00:01:11,995
Zarzycki agreed that notation was useful.

16
00:01:12,615 --> 00:01:18,565
He questioned giving the same symbol different meanings based on its position.

17
00:01:19,895 --> 00:01:25,045
Swift 2.2, released in March 2016, warned about these operators.

18
00:01:25,665 --> 00:01:29,635
To guide a migration, the compiler still had to recognize the old spelling.

19
00:01:30,255 --> 00:01:35,845
In April Lattner changed the warning into an error and kept migration diagnostics.

20
00:01:36,465 --> 00:01:42,195
That September, Swift 3 removed all four increment and decrement forms.

21
00:01:43,545 --> 00:01:45,795
The opening line saved four.

22
00:01:46,415 --> 00:01:47,885
First, we save four.

23
00:01:48,505 --> 00:01:52,025
Then the original variable changes to five.

24
00:01:52,615 --> 00:01:58,405
You used to have to check which side the plus signs were on to know the order.

25
00:01:59,025 --> 00:02:06,445
Swift removed these operators so the next reader could reach the same answer simply by reading the two lines in order.
